Researcher Eligibility
Access to restricted data on Databrary is limited to researchers who are formally authorized by their institutions via the Databrary Access Agreement (DAA) and its Annexes. Only academic, research, government, or health entities are eligible to authorize researchers under the DAA. Institutions must also maintain or use an ethics or Institutional Review Board with U.S. equivalent standards that reviews and approves research involving human subjects (or non-human animals where relevant).
An official signature by an Authorized Organizational Representative (AOR) on the DAA constitutes formal institutional approval.
Once authorized by their institution, Databrary considers the researchers authorized researchers. While institutions decide whether to authorize a specific researcher, the following criteria typically apply:

Eligible researchers conduct independent, unsupervised, research at the institution;
Eligible researchers submit funding proposals as lead investigator;
Eligible researchers have Principal Investigator (PI) status or hold a tenure-track faculty position at the institution;
Eligible researchers have current training that addresses research ethics with human subject (e.g. CITI training);
Eligible researchers wish to download and/or reuse or upload data to Databrary.

Authorized researchers often supervise the research activities of research staff, postdocs, graduate students, and undergraduate students who are not eligible for access to Databrary as authorized researchers. Consistent with institutional policies, these individuals may be granted access to Databrary as a supervised researcher of a supervising authorized researcher.

What is an authorized researcher?
An authorized researcher is a researcher with PI status or equivalent at their institution. Authorized researchers can authorize research staff, post-docs, or students as supervised researchers. Only authorized researchers and their designated manager can create volumes. Designated managers can suggest changes to collaborators on their supervising researcher’s volumes but only authorized researchers can manage collaborators on their volumes. Only authorized researchers can share volumes on Databrary.


Eligibility
Databrary policies and your home institution determine whether you are eligible to be granted access to Databrary as an authorized researcher. To be eligible as a Databrary authorized researcher, you must meet these criteria:

Conduct independent, unsupervised, research at your institution;
Submit funding proposals as lead investigator;
Have Principal Investigator (PI) status or hold a tenure-track faculty position at your institution;
Your institution maintains or uses an ethics board to review and approve research involving human subjects (or non-human animals where relevant);
Have current training that addresses research ethics with human subjects (e.g. CITI training);
Would like to reuse or upload data to Databrary.
Sponsor and supervise lab staff and students who work on your projects or on collaborative projects, and have authority to supervise their access to data that you share or others have shared on Databrary.



